#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193812 :tutorial/content "Two lines are **parallel** if they never meet. So, for example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are parallel.\n\n![](images/d9/d9bcd3cb4e201292afd4c3dca68273f16cff43d76c5a6a2f80348f5d8db1bd4c.png)\n\nIf two lines are parallel, we can express this using the $\\parallel$ symbol as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\parallel s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is parallel to the line $s$.\"\n\nIf two lines are not parallel, they must have a common point. In this case, we say that the lines **intersect**.\n\nFor example, the lines $v$ and $w$ below intersect at the point $P{:}$\n\n![](images/a6/a659c4a23e60880656c4fea0ec8e6ca63e0d9e55704fdb53b5bdf1a6e30b89e3.png)\n\nWe have the following important property about lines in two-dimensional space.\n\n> *Any two distinct lines are either parallel or intersect at exactly one point.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5d86f95-6437-58f6-a35f-cf72da79271b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193847 :tutorial/content "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect and form a $90^\\circ$ angle.\n\nFor example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are perpendicular.\n\n![](images/73/73d2e836e942d6e468c8d9652921a9321b842d47b54ba59529123da61a9a96b4.png)\n\nWhen two lines are perpendicular, we can express this with the symbol $\\perp$, as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\perp s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is perpendicular to the line $s$.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "2580c600-ddf7-5d7e-983d-e971ce3c6700" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6653 :tutorial/title "Perpendicular Lines"}]}
